+# Cartesian → polar position conversion
+
+How OAR converts object positions supplied as room-cube cartesian
+coordinates (ADM-style, x/y/z ∈ [−1, 1]) into the polar positions the
+renderer libraries consume. Two modes, selected per OAR instance:
+
+```c
+oar_set_position_conversion(oar, ck_oar_pos_conv_layout);  /* opt in  */
+oar_get_position_conversion(oar);          /* ck_oar_pos_conv_sector by
+                                              default                  */
+```
+
+Polar-typed metadata is never affected. The mode may be flipped between
+render calls (standard threading contract).
+
+## `ck_oar_pos_conv_sector` (default)
+
+The BS.2127 §10 sector map with its fixed five-anchor table — fronts at
+±30°, surrounds at ±110° — regardless of the output layout
+(`cartesian_to_polar_sector_float32()`, byte-for-byte upstream code).
+On layouts whose speakers sit elsewhere (7.1.4's ±90/±135 mid ring and
+±45 tops, 9.1.6's wides), cube walls and corners land between speakers
+and render as phantom images.
+
+## `ck_oar_pos_conv_layout`
+
+The same sector machinery, but the azimuth anchors are derived from the
+output layout's own speakers (`src/position_conversion.c`), so cube
+walls and corners pin to real speakers on every layout.
+
+### Anchor derivation (once, at `oar_create()`)
+
+From the OLR speaker table of the target layout, excluding LFE and
+zenith/nadir (|el| ≈ 90°):
+
+1. **Cluster by elevation** into rings: mid (|el| ≤ 5°), top (el > 5°),
+   bottom (el < −5°).
+2. **Map each speaker azimuth to its room-cube wall point** (the
+   BS.2127 allocentric convention, with corner snapping): 0° → (0, 1);
+   ±30° and ±45° → front corners (∓1, 1); ±60° → side wall at
+   (∓1, 0.414214); ±90° → (∓1, 0); ±110° and ±135° → rear corners
+   (∓1, −1); 180° → (0, −1). Azimuths outside this table (the ±25°
+   screen speakers of sound system G) use the generic rule — the
+   inverse §10 map at el = 0, d = 1, which is BS.2127's own placement
+   for screen speakers (±25° ≈ (∓0.87, 1)).
+3. **Complete rings that don't close behind the listener**: a ring with
+   no anchor beyond ±90° gets phantom rear corners keyed off its front
+   pair — fronts at ±45° pair with ±135° rears, fronts at ±30° (and the
+   no-front-pair fallback) with the standard ±110°. The mid ring
+   additionally gets a front-centre anchor when the layout has no
+   centre speaker (stereo). This reproduces the fixed table exactly for
+   mono/stereo/5.1.
+
+Duplicate azimuths dedupe circularly; a top or bottom ring with fewer
+than three anchors is dropped.
+
+### Conversion (per position)
+
+- **Azimuth**: sector solve over the mid ring — find the sector
+  containing the position, solve the 2×2 gain system against the two
+  anchor wall points, map the gain ratio through the §10 tan law
+  between the anchor azimuths. If the layout has a top ring and z > 0
+  (or a bottom ring and z < 0), the same solve runs over that ring and
+  the azimuth blends toward it along the shortest circular path by
+  t = min(|z|, 1) — at the cube's top surface the top ring's map holds
+  outright, so top corners pin to the real height speakers.
+- **Elevation and distance**: unchanged from §10. With
+  el̃ = atan(z / r_xy): |el̃| ≤ 45° maps linearly to ±30° with
+  d = r_xy; beyond 45° it maps 45°..90° → 30°..90° with d = |z| (cube
+  top edges land exactly on the 30° height ring).
+- **Binaural and mono outputs** have no speaker ring to anchor to and
+  use the non-warped *balloon* conversion instead: azimuth/elevation
+  straight from the position vector, distance = max(|x|, |y|, |z|), so
+  the cube surface inflates onto the unit sphere and interior points
+  keep their depth.
+
+If anchor derivation failed at creation (possible only for layouts
+unknown to the derivation — none today), a warning is logged and the
+layout mode behaves like the default.
+
+## Standalone conversion helpers (no `oar_t`)
+
+For hosts that convert positions outside an `oar_t` render loop —
+baking polar metadata for a renderer driven directly, e.g. binaural via
+the OBR C API or an ambisonic bed via SH encode — the same conversion is
+public in
+`include/oar.h`:
+
+```c
+oar_position_anchors_t *anchors =
+    oar_position_anchors_create(ck_oar_layout_714);  /* once per layout */
+/* On failure (not a target layout, derivation failed) anchors is NULL:
+ * mirror the in-renderer fallback by converting in sector mode. Don't
+ * pass the NULL on in layout mode — that selects the balloon conversion
+ * (see below), not the sector fallback. */
+oar_position_conversion_t mode =
+    anchors ? ck_oar_pos_conv_layout : ck_oar_pos_conv_sector;
+cartesian_t c = def_cartesian_instance(1.f, 1.f, 0.f); /* front-right corner */
+polar_t p = oar_cartesian_to_polar(anchors, mode, c);
+oar_position_anchors_destroy(anchors);
+```
+
+`oar_position_anchors_create()` runs the same derivation `oar_create()`
+runs for its target layout (binaural and mono handles select the
+balloon conversion) and returns NULL for non-target layouts; the handle
+is immutable and thread-safe to share. `oar_cartesian_to_polar()` is a
+pure function: with mode `ck_oar_pos_conv_sector` it applies the fixed
+§10 map (anchors ignored, may be NULL); with `ck_oar_pos_conv_layout` it
+applies the handle's anchored map, or — with a NULL handle — the
+balloon conversion. Every path is bit-identical to what an `oar_t` with
+the same target layout and mode applies to cartesian object metadata.
+
+## Guarantees, pinned by `tests/examples/test_position_conversion.c`
+
+- Default mode is bit-identical to upstream (it *is* upstream code, and
+  a set-and-revert of the mode leaves rendered output bit-identical).
+- On layouts whose derived rings equal the fixed table and which have
+  no height ring (stereo, 5.1), the layout mode is **bit-identical** to
+  the default — same shared §10 helper functions, same operation order.
+- Derived rings match the pan-law study's validated constants (panlab)
+  for the nine study layouts; ring sizes are pinned for 7.1.5.4,
+  10.2.9.3, and sound systems E/F/G.
+- A 675-point cube grid agrees with a reference dump generated from
+  `panlab-core.js` (double precision) to < 2 × 10⁻⁵ degrees.
+- On 7.1.4, cube corners pin 100% of their energy onto the
+  corresponding speakers (Rrs/Lrs/Rtf/Ltb).
+- The standalone public helpers are bit-identical to the internal
+  conversion on every supported target layout and in every mode, and
+  rendering their polar output as polar metadata is bit-identical to
+  letting the renderer convert the cartesian position.
+
+## Status and caveats
+
+Implements the recommendation of the pan-law study
+(`adm-lab`). Not yet validated by listening:
+bottom-ring blending (7.1.5.4 / 10.2.9.3 / sound system E extend the
+study's convention below the horizon) and the balloon distance's
+interaction with OBR's distance handling. Evaluate both before any
+upstream submission.
